Chapter: LensModel

 A Lens model maps raw warehouse tables to governed business concepts.
 Where Looker describes this mapping in LookML that only Looker can
 check, a Lens model is ordinary Codex data: records and lists that the
 type system checks like any other value. This chapter is the modeling
 vocabulary. It declares the shapes a model is made of and the
 validator that every model must pass before the planner will touch it.
 The planner itself lives in a later chapter; the query request shape
 it consumes is declared here so both sides agree on the contract.

Section: Cardinality

 A join declares its cardinality, and the declaration is load-bearing.
 The grain checker uses cardinality to decide whether a join can fan
 rows out and therefore whether a naive aggregate would double-count.
 ManyToOne is the workhorse: many fact rows point at one dimension row,
 and an aggregate computed at the fact table's own grain stays correct
 across the join. The other two directions are declared now and
 exploited by the planner later.

  Cardinality =
    | OneToOne
    | ManyToOne
    | OneToMany

Section: Aggregation

 Every measure names the aggregate that summarizes it. The five kinds
 below cover the LookML core. The constructors carry a Lens prefix
 rather than the bare names AggSum through AggMax because the Data
 quire's RelAlgebra chapter already owns those constructor names, and
 constructor names are program-wide: the planner will cite both
 chapters in one program, so the bare names would collide.

  AggKind =
    | LensAggSum
    | LensAggCount
    | LensAggAvg
    | LensAggMin
    | LensAggMax

Section: Tables

 A LensTable points the model at one physical table. The primary key
 is what makes grain reasoning possible: a many-to-one join is only
 trustworthy when the one side can be identified row by row. The grain
 field is prose for the analyst, one honest sentence about what a
 single row means.

  LensTable = record {
    name : Text,
    primary-key : Text,
    grain : Text
  }

Section: Joins

 A LensJoin relates two declared tables by one column on each side.
 The cardinality field is not documentation. The validator checks that
 a many-to-one join's destination declares a primary key, and the
 grain checker will refuse plans that fan out through it.

  LensJoin = record {
    from-table : Text,
    to-table : Text,
    on-left : Text,
    on-right : Text,
    cardinality : Cardinality
  }

Section: Time Buckets

 A dimension may bucket a date column by calendar period: year,
 quarter, month, or day. Dates are Unix seconds stored in integer
 columns by convention, and declaring a bucket is the modeler's
 assertion that the column holds Unix seconds; the validator does
 not police that assertion, in the same manner it does not police
 that an amount column holds cents. The constructors carry an Lb
 prefix because constructor names are program-wide and the bare
 calendar words are too valuable to claim. LbNone means the
 dimension reads its column as-is, which is every dimension that
 existed before buckets did.

  LensBucket =
    | LbNone
    | LbYear
    | LbQuarter
    | LbMonth
    | LbDay

Section: Dimensions

 A dimension is an attribute column that segments the data. It names
 its home table, the column it reads, and the bucket that column is
 read through.

  LensDimension = record {
    name : Text,
    table : Text,
    column : Text,
    bucket : LensBucket
  }

Section: Measures

 A measure is a governed aggregate: a column on a home table, an
 aggregation kind, and a description that a controller can sign off
 on. Measure filters are deferred. A filter needs a predicate type,
 and inventing one here would prejudge the planner's expression
 language. When the predicate type exists, filters become one more
 field on this record.

  LensMeasure = record {
    name : Text,
    table : Text,
    column : Text,
    agg : AggKind,
    description : Text
  }

Section: The Model

 A LensModel gathers the whole vocabulary under one name. This value
 is the unit of validation, the unit of versioning, and the ground the
 planner stands on.

  LensModel = record {
    name : Text,
    tables : List LensTable,
    joins : List LensJoin,
    dimensions : List LensDimension,
    measures : List LensMeasure
  }

Section: Filters

 A query filter is an equality test: one column on one table must
 equal one literal value. The value is its own small sum so the
 filter mirrors the relational predicate's column-versus-literal
 shape without citing the engine: an integer or a text, nothing
 else in v1. The planner places each filter at raw-row grain, under
 the aggregations, therefore a filter can never see a summed row.

  LensFilterValue =
    | LfInt (Integer)
    | LfText (Text)

  LensFilter = record {
    table : Text,
    column : Text,
    value : LensFilterValue
  }

Section: Queries

 A LensQuery is the request shape the planner consumes: which lens to
 ask, which dimensions to group by, which measures to compute, the
 filters to apply before any aggregation, and a row limit. Dimension
 and measure names refer to the model's declared dimensions and
 measures, never to raw columns; filters name physical tables and
 columns and the planner checks them against the model's vocabulary.

  LensQuery = record {
    lens-name : Text,
    dimensions : List Text,
    measures : List Text,
    filters : List LensFilter,
    limit : Integer
  }

Section: Diagnostic Codes

 Validation reports diagnostics as code plus message. Codes are
 app-level, drawn from the 1 to 99 range, and are not compiler CDX
 codes. The table:

 code 1 means a table name is declared more than once.
 code 2 means a join's from-table is not a declared table.
 code 3 means a join's to-table is not a declared table.
 code 4 means a many-to-one join's to-table declares no primary key.
 code 5 means a dimension's table is not a declared table.
 code 6 means a measure's table is not a declared table.
 code 7 means a measure name is declared more than once.

  LensDiag = record {
    code : Integer,
    message : Text
  }

  lens-diag : Integer, Text -> LensDiag
  lens-diag (code) (msg) = LensDiag { code = code, message = msg }

Section: Table Lookup

 Two small scans over the declared tables. Both walk by index with an
 explicit stop, so the duplicate check can reuse the same walk to ask
 whether a name appears before a given position.

  table-name-within : List LensTable, Integer, Integer, Text -> Boolean
  table-name-within (ts) (i) (stop) (nm) =
    if i >= stop then False
    else let t = list-at ts i
    in if t.name == nm then True
    else table-name-within ts (i + 1) stop nm

  table-declared : List LensTable, Text -> Boolean
  table-declared (ts) (nm) = table-name-within ts 0 (list-length ts) nm

  table-pk-within : List LensTable, Integer, Text -> Text
  table-pk-within (ts) (i) (nm) =
    if i >= list-length ts then ""
    else let t = list-at ts i
    in if t.name == nm then t.primary-key
    else table-pk-within ts (i + 1) nm

  table-pk : List LensTable, Text -> Text
  table-pk (ts) (nm) = table-pk-within ts 0 nm

Section: Validation

 lens-validate is the gate between a model as written and a model the
 planner may plan against. It threads one diagnostic accumulator
 through four passes: duplicate tables, joins, dimensions, measures.
 A clean model yields the empty list.

  lens-validate : LensModel -> List LensDiag
  lens-validate (model) =
    let d1 = check-table-dups (model.tables) 0 []
    in let d2 = check-joins (model.tables) (model.joins) 0 d1
    in let d3 = check-dimensions (model.tables) (model.dimensions) 0 d2
    in check-measures (model.tables) (model.measures) 0 d3

  check-table-dups : List LensTable, Integer, List LensDiag -> List LensDiag
  check-table-dups (ts) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= list-length ts then acc
    else let t = list-at ts i
    in if table-name-within ts 0 i (t.name)
       then check-table-dups ts (i + 1) (list-push acc (table-dup-diag t))
       else check-table-dups ts (i + 1) acc

  table-dup-diag : LensTable -> LensDiag
  table-dup-diag (t) = lens-diag 1 ("duplicate table name " & t.name)

Section: Join Validation

 Each join is checked three ways: its from-table must be declared, its
 to-table must be declared, and when the join is many-to-one its
 to-table must carry a nonempty primary key, because grain checking
 identifies the one side by that key. The key check is skipped when
 the to-table is itself undeclared; code 3 already tells that story.

  check-joins : List LensTable, List LensJoin, Integer, List LensDiag -> List LensDiag
  check-joins (ts) (js) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= list-length js then acc
    else let j = list-at js i
    in let a1 = check-join-from ts j acc
    in let a2 = check-join-to ts j a1
    in let a3 = check-join-key ts j a2
    in check-joins ts js (i + 1) a3

  check-join-from : List LensTable, LensJoin, List LensDiag -> List LensDiag
  check-join-from (ts) (j) (acc) =
    if table-declared ts (j.from-table) then acc
    else list-push acc (lens-diag 2 ("join from-table " & j.from-table & " is not a declared table"))

  check-join-to : List LensTable, LensJoin, List LensDiag -> List LensDiag
  check-join-to (ts) (j) (acc) =
    if table-declared ts (j.to-table) then acc
    else list-push acc (lens-diag 3 ("join to-table " & j.to-table & " is not a declared table"))

  check-join-key : List LensTable, LensJoin, List LensDiag -> List LensDiag
  check-join-key (ts) (j) (acc) =
    when j.cardinality
      is ManyToOne -> check-join-pk ts j acc
      is otherwise -> acc

  check-join-pk : List LensTable, LensJoin, List LensDiag -> List LensDiag
  check-join-pk (ts) (j) (acc) =
    if table-declared ts (j.to-table)
    then if table-pk ts (j.to-table) /= "" then acc
         else list-push acc (lens-diag 4 ("many-to-one join to " & j.to-table & " has no primary key"))
    else acc

Section: Dimension and Measure Validation

 Dimensions and measures must live on declared tables, and measure
 names must be unique because the query surface addresses measures by
 name alone.

  check-dimensions : List LensTable, List LensDimension, Integer, List LensDiag -> List LensDiag
  check-dimensions (ts) (ds) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= list-length ds then acc
    else let d = list-at ds i
    in let a1 = if table-declared ts (d.table) then acc else list-push acc (dim-table-diag d)
    in check-dimensions ts ds (i + 1) a1

  dim-table-diag : LensDimension -> LensDiag
  dim-table-diag (d) = lens-diag 5 ("dimension " & d.name & " names undeclared table " & d.table)

  measure-name-within : List LensMeasure, Integer, Integer, Text -> Boolean
  measure-name-within (ms) (i) (stop) (nm) =
    if i >= stop then False
    else let m = list-at ms i
    in if m.name == nm then True
    else measure-name-within ms (i + 1) stop nm

  check-measures : List LensTable, List LensMeasure, Integer, List LensDiag -> List LensDiag
  check-measures (ts) (ms) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= list-length ms then acc
    else let m = list-at ms i
    in let a1 = if table-declared ts (m.table) then acc else list-push acc (measure-table-diag m)
    in let a2 = if measure-name-within ms 0 i (m.name) then list-push a1 (measure-dup-diag m) else a1
    in check-measures ts ms (i + 1) a2

  measure-table-diag : LensMeasure -> LensDiag
  measure-table-diag (m) = lens-diag 6 ("measure " & m.name & " names undeclared table " & m.table)

  measure-dup-diag : LensMeasure -> LensDiag
  measure-dup-diag (m) = lens-diag 7 ("duplicate measure name " & m.name)
